---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Feature from '../components/Feature.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout title="What's new" description="The changes of Civ 1.5 Remastered: unit health, healing, railroads, saves, Load Game, sound setup, fixes.">
  <h1>What's new</h1>
  <p class="lead">The big changes first, the smaller fixes after them.</p>

  <Feature
    id="unit-health"
    title="Units have health"
    caption="Health bars over the units, all over the map."
    image="unit-health.png"
  >
    <p>
      A unit has 3 hit points, a veteran 4, and an elite unit 5. Damage stays after the battle, and a health bar
      shows it on the map and during the battle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Battles are fought in rounds: each round removes one hit point from the side that lost it. The original decided
      a battle with a single roll, so a Trireme could sink a Battleship; now a weak unit has to win several rounds in a
      row.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hit points are saved in a small file next to the saved game (<code>CIVIL0.HP</code> next to
      <code>CIVIL0.SVE</code>), so the original game can still load the save.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="load-game"
    title="Load Game in the game menu"
    caption="Load Game in the GAME menu."
    image="load-game.png"
  >
    <p>
      The game menu has <strong>Load Game</strong>, so you no longer need to quit to load a save. The load list shows
      the autosaves, newest first, or your own saved games.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="saves"
    title="10 save slots and 20 autosaves"
    caption="The 20 autosaves, one for every turn."
    image="saves.png"
    secondCaption="The 10 slots for your own saved games."
    secondImage="saved-games.png"
  >
    <p>
      Save your games in 10 slots (<code>CIVIL0</code> – <code>CIVIL9</code>) instead of 4. On top of that the game
      saves itself at every turn into 20 autosaves (<code>AUTO00</code> – <code>AUTO19</code>), the oldest one replaced
      first, so you can always go back up to 20 turns. The original had 4 autosaves, made only every 1000 years.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="wonders"
    title="Wonders never become obsolete"
    caption="Memphis with the Hanging Gardens, still making people happy."
    image="wonders.png"
  >
    <p>
      In the original most Wonders of the World stopped working once somebody discovered a later technology: the
      Hanging Gardens with Invention, the Pyramids with Communism, the Great Library with University. Now every Wonder
      works for as long as you hold its city.
    </p>
    <p>The Civilopedia describes the Wonders without the "until the development of" notes.</p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="upgrades"
    title="Upgrade units for gold"
    caption="Upgrade to Phalanx in the Orders menu of a Militia in Thebes."
    image="upgrades.png"
    secondCaption="The Civilopedia tells what a unit can be upgraded to, and for how much."
    secondImage="upgrades-civilopedia.png"
  >
    <p>
      An old unit standing in one of your cities can be upgraded to its successor once you know the technology:
      <strong>Orders → Upgrade</strong>, or the <code>E</code> key. The unit keeps its veteran or elite rank and its
      damage, and it uses up its moves for the turn.
    </p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li>Cavalry → Chariot → Knights → Armor</li>
      <li>Militia → Phalanx → Musketeers → Riflemen → Mech. Inf.</li>
      <li>Legion → Musketeers</li>
      <li>Catapult → Cannon → Artillery</li>
      <li>Trireme → Sail → Frigate → Transport</li>
      <li>Ironclad → Cruiser → Battleship</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The price is the price of buying the missing shields in a city, at least $30: for example $30 from Phalanx to
      Musketeers, $160 from Knights to Armor, $480 from Cruiser to Battleship. The computer players upgrade their units
      too.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="railroads"
    title="Railroads"
    caption="Railroads running into Thebes and on through the city."
    image="railroads.png"
  >
    <p>
      A city has a railroad once its owner knows Railroad. In the original a city only had a road, so a train lost a
      third of its moves in every city it crossed.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="sound-upgrade"
    title="Authentic sound selection & Tandy 1000 driver"
    caption="Authentic DOS hardware setup screen: select graphics, sound, and controls."
    image="sound-mode-select.png"
  >
    <p>
      The game starts with authentic 1991 setup screens rendered in 640×400 with IBM PC ROM BIOS fonts.
      Features newly engineered cycle-accurate emulation for Tandy 1000 3-voice sound (TI SN76496)
      and real-time Roland MT-32 translation to General MIDI.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read all about the audio systems on the dedicated <a href={`${base}/sound/`}>Sound & Music page</a>.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="demographics"
    title="Demographics"
    caption="The demographics of a large empire: approval and productivity counted right."
    image="demographics.png"
  >
    <p>
      The demographics screen counted with 16-bit numbers and overflowed in large empires, showing negative approval,
      productivity and disease. It now counts correctly.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <h2>More changes</h2>

  <div class="change-grid">
    <section class="change-card" id="city-projects">
      <h3>City Projects (Wealth, Research, Food)</h3>
      <p>
        Instead of building units or improvements, cities can continuously convert industrial production into revenue, science, or food:
      </p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li><strong>Wealth:</strong> converts 50% of net shields directly into city taxes and gold (unlocked with Banking).</li>
        <li><strong>Research:</strong> converts 50% of net shields directly into city science (unlocked with University).</li>
        <li><strong>Food:</strong> converts 50% of net shields directly into city food supplies (unlocked with Engineering).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="change-card" id="elite-units">
      <h3>Elite units</h3>
      <p>A veteran that wins a battle becomes elite and gets a fifth hit point.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="healing">
      <h3>Healing</h3>
      <p>Units heal every turn:</p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li>2 hit points in an own city, for every unit;</li>
        <li>1 hit point for a fortified land unit outside cities;</li>
        <li>1 hit point for a resting ship next to land, at most 3 tiles from an own city;</li>
        <li>aircraft heal only in cities.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="goto-railroads">
      <h3>GoTo on railroads</h3>
      <p>
        Moves along railroads are free also with GoTo and for the computer players. In the original only units moved
        by hand rode for free. Computer units get at most 30 free steps a turn, so nothing circles forever.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="sea-wonders">
      <h3>Lighthouse and Magellan add up</h3>
      <p>
        Each of the two Wonders gives ships one more move, so with both your ships get two. The original gave only one
        move even with both.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="boarding">
      <h3>Boarding a ship ends the moves</h3>
      <p>
        A land unit that boards a ship goes to sentry with no moves left, so it can no longer step on and off the ship
        in the same turn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="help-key">
      <h3>Alt+H always helps</h3>
      <p>
        Alt+H shows the help of the turn at any time. The original showed it only with the help option on and before
        the year 0.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="pillage">
      <h3>Pillage</h3>
      <p>Pillaging next to a foreign city no longer corrupts the game's memory.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="random-numbers">
      <h3>Better random numbers</h3>
      <p>
        The random numbers come from xoshiro128**, a fast modern generator, instead of the weak generator of the
        original.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="missing-files">
      <h3>Help with the game files</h3>
      <p>
        When the <code>CIVILIZATION</code> folder is missing, the game shows where to put it and where to find the
        original game, instead of closing.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>Smaller fixes</h2>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li>The palace style preview is drawn correctly.</li>
    <li>The game no longer freezes after a mouse click, and no longer crashes at the end of a turn.</li>
    <li>The arrow keys work in menus.</li>
    <li>Correct palette and screen in the window.</li>
    <li>The title logo, a new building in a city, the palace and the replay dissolve onto the screen again.</li>
    <li>The original AdLib and Roland MT-32 music, played through an emulated sound card and General MIDI.</li>
    <li>The game starts in a maximized window; F11 switches to full screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Fixes of places where the C# translation behaved differently from the DOS game are listed
    <a href={`${base}/translation-fixes/`}>on a separate page</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>
